feat(hook): retire a killed agent at the moment it is killed (#290)

A killed subagent fires no SubagentStop. Its marker survives, its work
survives, and the ledger goes on reporting the run active for the whole
of the reaper's grace -- measured, five runs Active for between three
and nine hours after the processes were gone.

A kill and a silence are different events and the system treated them as
one. A kill is certain: something asked for the agent to stop and was
told it had, so the run leaves the active list now. Silence is
ambiguous: the reaper's grace answers that one, and nothing here touches
it. No clock is read and no threshold is added.

PostToolUse now recognises a TaskStop the harness reports as having
stopped a local agent, maps its task_id to that agent's marker, and
retires the run through the same observe_session stop call the stop path
already makes. run_retired is the honest event -- the run did stop.

Five guards, each of which is a case:

  the RESULT, not the input -- a TaskStop that stopped nothing, named
  another task, or reported an error retires nothing
  the task kind -- a stopped background shell is not a run
  the marker -- a task_id with no marker retires nothing, silently
  the shape -- a task_id is a marker name, never a path, and never the
  operator's own session marker
  the owner -- markers record which harness session started the run, and
  another session's run is never reached

The marker is removed, which is the local half of the double-retirement
guard rather than a new one: a stop arriving afterwards takes the
absent-marker exit it has always had, and the lifecycle tool answers a
repeated stop from the instant of the first (#179).

The stop-time capture is deliberately not run here. This is the parent's
own tool call, mid-flight, and signing another run's index from there is
#261 with a new trigger. What is left instead is the durable record
#277 writes, so the orphaned work is findable (#288).

Driven by OPS-096, OPS-097, OPS-098, OPS-099 and OPS-100 in
scripts/hooks/shim-selftest.sh, written first and observed failing
against the unchanged shim -- twelve assertions red, each with its own
positive control at zero so the absence proves the discriminator rather
than the feature being missing.

Verified against the live ledger: a run registered through the hook,
seen active, killed through a synthetic PostToolUse in the measured
payload shape, and gone from status=active with the ledger reporting
retired. Exactly one run was retired. A real background-shell kill
replayed against the real markers directory retired nothing and said
nothing.

One consequence worth naming: the destructive-git guard asks the ledger
who is live, so a killed run stops protecting its tree as soon as it is
retired. That protection was a side effect of the run being wrongly
active. The record is what replaces it.

